{"causes":["George Orwell's personal experiences during World War II influenced his writing of Animal Farm","Orwell's growing disillusionment with socialism and communism led him to create an allegory for the Russian Revolution","The publication of The Great Gatsby by F. Scott Fitzgerald in 1925 inspired Orwell's use of literary devices to critique societal norms"],"effects":["Animal Farm has been translated into over 70 languages, sold millions of copies worldwide, and adapted into numerous stage productions and films","The novella's exploration of totalitarianism and corruption has influenced generations of writers and thinkers, including philosophers like Hannah Arendt and political scientists like Fareed Zakaria","Orwell's critique of Stalinism and the Soviet Union's rise to power remains relevant today, as similar authoritarian regimes continue to pose threats to global democracy"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"George Orwell","sourceName":"George Orwell","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Animal Farm","targetName":"Animal Farm","context":"Orwell wrote the novella while recovering from tuberculosis in his brother's home in the English countryside"},{"sourceSlug":"Russian Revolution","sourceName":"Russian Revolution","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Animal Farm","targetName":"Animal Farm","context":"The revolution's ideals of equality and justice served as a backdrop for Orwell's critique of Stalinism and the rise of totalitarianism"},{"sourceSlug":"F. Scott Fitzgerald","sourceName":"F. Scott Fitzgerald","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Animal Farm","targetName":"Animal Farm","context":"Fitzgerald's The Great Gatsby inspired Orwell's use of literary devices to critique societal norms and the corrupting influence of power"}],"places":[{"name":"England","role":"Home country"},{"name":"Moscow","role":"Capital city of Soviet Union"},{"name":"London","role":"Metropolis"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Animal Farm","type":"Reference article","slug":"animal-farm"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1396889","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_Farm"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Animal Farm left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 120 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Animal Farm is the political allegory by George Orwell (Eric Arthur Blair, 1903–1950), published in August 1945 by Secker & Warburg — one of the most widely read political satires in world literature and one of Orwell's two masterpieces (alongside Nineteen Eighty-Four, 1949). Written in 1944 and rejected by multiple publishers (including T.S. Eliot at Faber, who found its anti-Soviet message politically inopportune), Animal Farm is a fairy story (as its subtitle calls it) in which the animals of Manor Farm, led by the pigs Napoleon and Snowball, overthrow their human farmer Mr Jones in the name of 'Animalism' (a transparent allegory for Marxism-Leninism) — and then watch as the pigs gradually assume all the privileges of the humans they replaced, culminating in Napoleon's tyranny and the corruption of the Seven Commandments of Animalism to 'All animals are equal, but some animals are more equal than others.'

Animal Farm is a precise political allegory of the Soviet Union from the 1917 Revolution to the Tehran Conference of 1943: Old Major is Marx/Lenin (the ideological founder whose vision is betrayed after his death), Napoleon is Stalin, Snowball is Trotsky (expelled from the farm/USSR and blamed for all subsequent problems), the pigs are the Communist Party, the dogs are the secret police (NKVD), Boxer the horse is the proletariat (loyal, hardworking, ultimately exploited and destroyed), and the farm's corruption mirrors the Soviet Union's descent from revolutionary idealism into Stalinist tyranny. Squealer (the propagandist pig who rewrites history and manipulates the other animals) is one of the most chilling portrayals of propaganda and political manipulation in literature.

Animal Farm has been translated into more than 70 languages and has sold tens of millions of copies worldwide — it is one of the most effective anti-totalitarian texts ever written, its fable form making its critique of Soviet communism accessible to readers of all ages and levels of political sophistication. It was used as Cold War propaganda by Western governments (the CIA funded an animated film adaptation in 1954) and has remained continuously in print as a canonical text of political literacy.","subjects":["English Literature","Modern Era","George Orwell","Political Allegory","Anti-Totalitarianism","Soviet Union","20th Century","Dystopian Fiction"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Animal Farm offers a powerful warning about the dangers of totalitarianism and the importance of individual freedom and accountability in the face of ideological extremism.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
